325	398	Career
Andris Piebalgs was born on 17 September 1957 in Valmiera, Latvia.
399	463	Piebalgs is a graduate in Physics from the University of Latvia.
464	591	Andris Piebalgs is an experienced Latvian politician who occupied key positions in both national and European political fields.
592	775	Earlier in his career, while Latvia was still part of the Soviet Union, Andris Piebalgs worked as a teacher and was the headmaster of the 1st Secondary School of Valmiera (1980–1990).
776	860	He also served as director of the department in the Ministry of Education in Latvia.
861	923	He was a member of Communist Party of Soviet Union from 1979.

923	1026	In 1988, Latvian People's Front, an organisation that united more than 200,000 people, was established.
1027	1124	Supporters of Latvian self-determination gained victory in the March 1990 parliamentary election.
1125	1248	It was the first time since the Soviet occupation that candidates from various political parties were allowed to take part.
1249	1412	On 7 May 1990, Andris Piebalgs was appointed as Minister of Education in the new Government, and one year later, in August 1991, Latvia regained full independence.
1413	1515	Andris Piebalgs' main task was then to put in place the first independent education system in Latvia.

1515	1689	In 1993, Andris Piebalgs won a seat in the Latvian Parliament, when the first elections after the restoration of independence were held according to the Latvian election law.
1690	1763	He served as the chairman of the budget and finance committee until 1994.
1764	1849	In September 1994, he became Latvia's Minister of Finance and deputy prime minister.

1849	1915	From 1995 until 2003, he worked in the Latvian Diplomatic service.
1916	2066	Andris Piebalgs first served as the ambassador of Latvia in Estonia (1995–1997), where he helped to solve the sea border issue between the two states.
2067	2118	In 1997 he became the Latvian Ambassador to the EU.
2119	2297	In this capacity he helped to establish Latvia as the EU candidate country and led the accession negotiations, which resulted in Latvia's successful accession to the EU in 2004.

2297	2419	From 2003 to 2004, he was appointed Deputy Secretary of State for EU affairs at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Latvia.
2420	2533	His role was instrumental in coordinating Latvia's position in the European Council and all Council formations.  
2533	2616	At the time, he also established the new development assistance policy for Latvia.

2616	2743	In addition to his native Latvian he is fluent in English, German, French, and Russian, and has basic knowledge of Estonian.



